[%think 53528] [%out 54049] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 1368] **Idea:**
White's Qd2 and Rd1 line up against my queen on d8. My knight on c5 is
protected only by the d6 pawn. My bishop on c8 blocks the a8 rook, so nothing
else guards d8. - **Why this matters:** If the d4 knight moves away (for
example Ndb5 or Nb3), White can play Bxc5. If I recapture dxc5, the d-file
opens. Then Qxd8 Rxd8 Rxd8+ wins a whole rook. So I need to get my queen off
the d-file. - **Why Qe7:** - It removes the queen from the d-file battery. - It
adds a defender to d6 and c7. - Together with Re8 it pressures the e4 pawn. -
It prepares ...c6, ...Nfd7 and ...Ne5 later. - **Checking White's replies:** -
13.Nd5 Nxd5 14.exd5? loses a piece to ...Bxd4, because the e-file opens against
Be3 and Be2. 14.cxd5 is fine for me. - 13.Nb3 can be met by ...a4, which pushes
the knight back. - 13.Bg5, pinning the f6 knight, is best met by ...Ne6, which
attacks the bishop. I must not play ...h6, because Bxh6 Bxh6 Qxh6 wins a pawn
for White. - 13.Nf5 loses: after ...Bxf5 exf5, ...Qxe3+ wins the e3 bishop. }
