[%think 96127] [%out 96770] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 1460] **Threats
to assess:** 1. **a5 is hanging.** After Nc3 moved to d5 and was exchanged off,
the c3 and b4 squares are empty. The white queen on d2 now attacks a5 directly,
and nothing defends it. After 17.Qxa5 White gains a pawn, and the queen also
hits c7 through b6. 2. **The Bxc5 tactic.** If White plays Bxc5 and I recapture
dxc5, then d6 opens up. White's d5-d6 would fork c7 and my queen on e7. After
cxd6, White's knight recaptures on d6 and hits e8. **Candidates I rejected:** -
**Nfxd5?** White replies 17.Qxd5 and keeps the extra knight. I lose a piece for
a pawn. - **Nfd7 / Ncd7** block the queen's defence of c7. Then Nxc7 wins a
pawn and attacks the rook on e8. - **Ra8** protects a5, but it removes the rook
from the d-file. Then Bxc5 dxc5 d6 leaves me in trouble. - **a4** moves the
pawn away, but Bxc5 dxc5 followed by bxa4 still costs a pawn. - **Knight
sacrifices on e4** give up a piece for two pawns, and the compensation isn't
enough. **Choice: 16...b6.** It defends a5, and it defends the c5 knight a
second time. If Bxc5, I can recapture bxc5 and keep the d6 pawn blocking the
passer. If I recapture dxc5, the d6 fork is also defusable: after cxd6 Nxd6, my
rook on d8 pins the knight to the queen on d2, and ...Re6 counterattacks it.
The cost is a hole on c6, which White's knight can use via d4. I can meet that
with ...Qd7 and an exchanging manoeuvre, and material stays level. } 17. Nd4
