[%think 25417] [%out 25806] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 1602] Choice:
recapture the bishop on c5, taking with the d6 pawn. **Material:** after the
recapture Black is up one pawn (queen, two rooks, bishop and 7 pawns against
queen, two rooks, knight and 6 pawns). **Tactics to rule out:** - **...Be3+
(in-between check):** after Bxe3 Rxe3 Qxe3, Black just loses the bishop. Not
good. - **bxc5:** a5 loses its defender, and White can win the pawn back
straight away with Nxa5. **Why dxc5:** - The queenside pawn chain a5-b6-c5
(plus c7) stays compact, and b6 protects both a5 and c5. - Black's queen gets
the f8-e7-d6 diagonal, so it can blockade on d6. - If White pushes d6, the
reply cxd6 settles it. The d6 pawn is then covered by the queen, and Black is
two pawns up. - Ne7+ just loses the knight to Qxe7, since nothing supports it.
- The rook on e2 keeps pressure on the second rank (it eyes a2 and g2). } 23.
